  "summary": "Mechanistic models now link circuit architecture and operating regime to inference reliability, pattern formation, and disease-relevant oscillation changes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that circuit behavior depends on how connectivity is architected and which dynamical regime the network occupies. A spatially constrained modular spiking-network study shows that spatial constraints and regime alter how well structural connectivity can be recovered from simulated activity, so inference from activity is conditional on regime. A two-layer neural field with differential interlayer coupling shows a Turing instability producing spatial patterns, offered as a theoretical mechanism relevant to Parkinson's disease and deep brain stimulation. A mean-field PN-PVI circuit model finds that persistent prefrontal gamma is more vulnerable to synaptic alterations than stimulus-locked V1 gamma, with implications for schizophrenia. The shared emphasis is on coupling structure and regime as determinants of observable circuit behavior. The items do not share a common method or system, so the link rests on the architecture-dynamics relationship rather than on shared tooling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
